---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { href, GITHUB, siteMeta, scorecard, census } from '../lib/corpus.js';
// Every number on this page is read from siteMeta()/scorecard()/census() — the
// same loaders the homepage tiles and footer use — so the definitions can't drift.
const m = siteMeta();
const sc = scorecard();
const cen = census();
const URLS = {
  'React Native Rewind': 'https://reactnativerewind.com/',
  'This Week in React': 'https://thisweekinreact.com/',
  'React Status': 'https://react.statuscode.com/',
  'React Digest': 'https://reactdigest.net/',
  'React Weekly': 'https://react-weekly.dev/',
  'Native Weekly': 'https://nativeweekly.com/',
};
const NEWSLETTERS = m.sources.map((s) => ({ ...s, url: URLS[s.name] || null }));
// calibration evaluation window: earliest assertion → latest resolution among GRADED predictions
const evalWindow = sc && sc.resolved.length ? {
  from: sc.resolved.map((r) => r.asserted).sort()[0],
  to: sc.resolved.map((r) => r.outcome.on).sort().at(-1),
} : null;
const tiers = sc ? ['high', 'medium', 'low'].map((t) => ({ t, ...sc.tiers[t] })) : [];
const graded = tiers.reduce((n, r) => n + r.resolved, 0);
const { bare, withCorpus, bankSize } = m.bench;
const runLine = (r) => `${r.freshPct}% fresh · ${r.confidentlyStalePct}% confidently stale · ${r.hedged}/${r.n} hedged (${r.model}, ${r.date}, n=${r.n})`;
---
<Base title="Methodology" description="How the corpus stays true: verification, adversarial challenge, calibration, live-npm signals, mechanized invariants.">
  <h1>Methodology — how this stays true</h1>
  <p class="prose">Most React resources rot silently: a listicle is true the day it's published and
  unaccountable after. react-brain is built around the opposite bet — <strong>every claim should be
  checkable, dated, and eventually scored.</strong> Five mechanisms do the work:</p>

  <h2>1. Verified facts, not vibes</h2>
  <p class="prose">Load-bearing facts (versions, maintenance status, breaking changes) are checked against <em>primary sources</em> — official blogs, changelogs, the npm registry — before they enter an entry, and
  stamped with a <code>verified:</code> date you can see on every page. Newsletter claims are treated as
  leads, never as facts.</p>

  <h2>2. Context-keyed recommendations</h2>
  <p class="prose">No entry says "X is best." Recommendations are written as <em>context → choice</em> clauses ("GraphQL backend → Apollo"; "P2P/serverless app → a client cache is N/A by design"). The same
  resolver that renders these pages powers the CLI and the <a href={href('doctor/')}>doctor</a>, so the
  advice can't drift between surfaces.</p>

  <h2>3. Adversarial challenge</h2>
  <p class="prose">High-stakes recommendations are periodically <em>attacked</em>: a challenger steelmans
  the case against the default and tests it with current evidence. Verdicts — SURVIVES / WEAKENED /
  OVERTURNED — require concrete evidence, and overturning demands the alternative win on a stated axis.
  Survived challenges are recorded on the entry; they're a stronger trust signal than editorial review.</p>

  <h2>4. Calibration — a published track record</h2>
  <p class="prose">Every default is logged as a dated, falsifiable prediction with a check-by horizon
  (shorter for fast-moving domains). Resolutions land in an append-only ledger and are scored per
  confidence tier on the <a href={href('scorecard/')}>scorecard</a>. Unresolved predictions are never
  counted as wins — "unproven" is displayed as unproven.</p>

  <h2>5. External anchors + mechanized invariants</h2>
  <p class="prose">Editorial opinion is checked against the outside world: live npm downloads and publish
  dates flag claims that data contradicts (a library called "maintenance mode" that shipped last week gets
  its claim weakened — that has actually happened, see the scorecard). Structural rules — every entry has
  vetted reading, every category is reachable, no duplicate sources, schemas hold — run as an offline lint
  plus a golden-fixture eval in <a href={`${GITHUB}/actions`}>CI on every change</a>.</p>

  <h2 id="metrics">Metrics, defined</h2>
  <p class="prose">Every figure this site prints is computed at build time from the corpus files —
  none is typed into copy. Here is what each one counts, with its current value.</p>
  <dl class="metrics">
    <dt id="m-decisions">decisions <span class="meta">{m.decisions}</span></dt>
    <dd>Entries in the encyclopedia — one per engineering decision (state, navigation, lists…).
      {m.reviewed} of {m.decisions} have status <em>reviewed</em>; the rest are drafted or stubs and say so on the page.</dd>

    <dt id="m-readings">readings <span class="meta">{m.readings}</span></dt>
    <dd>Reading annotations across all entries: an article, talk or doc that was fetch-verified
      (opened, read, still live) and given a one-line editorial note on why it matters. "Watching"
      items are not counted. See <a href={href('reading/')}>the reading index</a>.</dd>

    <dt id="m-libraries">libraries tracked <span class="meta">{m.libraries}</span></dt>
    <dd>Distinct npm package names in the entries' detection tables — the packages the doctor
      recognizes in a <code>package.json</code>. Wildcard patterns (e.g. a scope) are excluded, so
      this undercounts rather than inflates.</dd>

    <dt id="m-verified">last verified <span class="meta">{m.verifiedLatest}</span></dt>
    <dd>The most recent <code>updated:</code> date on any entry — the last time an entry's facts were
      checked against primary sources. {m.verifiedThisWeek} entries were re-verified in the 7 days
      before this build ({m.built}). <em>corpus changed</em> ({m.contentLatest}) is the last git
      commit touching entries or explanations.</dd>

    <dt id="m-calibration">calibration{m.calibration && <span class="meta"> {m.calibration.score}% {m.calibration.tier} tier ({m.calibration.graded}/{m.calibration.total} graded)</span>}</dt>
    <dd>
      Each <code>recommend.default</code> is logged as a dated prediction. When a challenge or signal
      resolves it, the outcome scores <strong>held = 1</strong>, <strong>weakened = 0.5</strong>,{' '}<strong>overturned = 0</strong>. A tier's score = (held + 0.5 × weakened) ÷ resolved predictions
      in that tier. Ungraded (unresolved) predictions are excluded from the denominator — never
      counted as wins — so always read a score with its n.
      {sc ? <>
        {' '}Evaluation window: {evalWindow ? <>assertions from {evalWindow.from}, resolutions through {evalWindow.to}</> : 'no resolutions yet'};
        {' '}{graded} of {sc.totalPredictions} predictions graded.
        <div class="tbl"><table>
          <thead><tr><th scope="col">tier</th><th scope="col" class="num">score</th><th scope="col" class="num">graded / predictions</th><th scope="col" class="num">held · weakened · overturned</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            {tiers.map((r) => (
              <tr><td>{r.t}</td><td class="num">{r.score != null ? `${r.score}%` : 'unproven'}</td><td class="num">{r.resolved} / {r.total}</td><td class="num">{r.held} · {r.weakened} · {r.overturned}</td></tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table></div>
        Full ledger on the <a href={href('scorecard/')}>scorecard</a>.
      </> : <> No predictions ledger is present in this build.</>}
    </dd>

    <dt id="m-sources">sources <span class="meta">{m.sourcesRunning} running · {m.sourcesRetired} retired</span></dt>
    <dd>Newsletters digested for leads (never facts — every fact is re-verified at its primary source).
      A source is retired when it stops originating leads the others don't already cover; its past
      digests stay counted. Current list under <a href="#sources">Sources &amp; thanks</a>.</dd>

    <dt id="m-census">census cohort <span class="meta">{m.censusApps != null ? `${m.censusApps} apps` : 'no snapshot'}</span></dt>
    <dd>{cen ? <>Shipped, production open-source apps whose manifests were read to observe actual
      adoption — {cen.cohort.rn} React Native, {cen.cohort.web} web, snapshot {m.censusDate}. It measures what
      shipped apps use, not what is best. See <a href={href('census/')}>census</a>.</> : <>No census snapshot is present in this build.</>}</dd>

    <dt id="m-bench">benchmark fresh% / confidently-stale%</dt>
    <dd>A bank of {bankSize} questions, each derived from a verified corpus fact, asked to a model
      with and without the relevant entry as context; graded by a deterministic regex rubric (no judge
      model). <strong>fresh%</strong> = answers that state the current truth ÷ n.{' '}<strong>confidently-stale%</strong> = answers asserting the outdated fact without any hedge ÷ n.
      n excludes API errors. Latest runs:
      <div class="bench-runs">
        bare: {bare ? runLine(bare) : 'no run'}<br />
        with corpus: {withCorpus ? runLine(withCorpus) : 'no run'}
      </div>
      See <a href={href('benchmark/')}>benchmark</a>.</dd>
  </dl>

  <h2>Editorial line on articles</h2>
  <p class="prose">Great articles are cited with a short editorial annotation on <em>why they matter</em> and linked prominently to the original. Nothing is republished or paraphrased at length — <a href={href('reading/')}>the reading index</a> exists to send you to the canonical source with
  context, not to replace it.</p>

  <h2 id="sources">Sources &amp; thanks</h2>
  <p class="prose">The corpus's discovery layer is newsletters, digested issue by issue —
  {m.sourcesRunning} running{m.sourcesRetired > 0 && <>, {m.sourcesRetired} retired after they stopped originating leads</>}.
  Our genuine thanks to the people who curate them week after week:</p>
  <div class="rows">
    {NEWSLETTERS.map((s) => {
      const label = [s.issues != null && `${s.issues} issues digested`, s.retired && `retired${s.retiredOn ? ` ${s.retiredOn}` : ''}`].filter(Boolean).join(' · ');
      const inner = <>
        <span class="row-title">{s.name}</span>
        {label && <span class="meta">{label}</span>}
      </>;
      return s.url
        ? <a class="row src" href={s.url} rel="noopener">{inner}</a>
        : <div class="src">{inner}</div>;
    })}
  </div>
  <p class="muted small prose">To be precise about the division of labor:
  newsletters surface the <em>leads</em> — every load-bearing fact is then independently
  fetch-verified against primary sources (release notes, changelogs, the npm registry) before it
  enters the corpus, and the newsletters have not reviewed or endorsed anything here. The same
  gratitude extends to every author in <a href={href('reading/')}>the reading index</a>, credited
  by name on their entries.</p>

  <p class="muted prose">Everything above is inspectable: the corpus, the tools, the ledger, and the tests are
  all in <a href={GITHUB}>the repo</a>.</p>
</Base>

<style>
  .prose { max-width: var(--measure); }
  .small { font-size: var(--fs-sm); }
  p.small { margin-top: var(--sp-3); }
  .src { display: flex; justify-content: space-between; gap: var(--sp-4); align-items: baseline; }
  .row-title { font-family: var(--font-head); font-weight: 600; color: var(--bright); }
  .metrics { margin: 1rem 0 2rem; border-top: 1px solid var(--line); }
  .metrics dt { font: 600 var(--fs-sm)/1.4 var(--font-mono); color: var(--bright); padding-top: .9rem; }
  .metrics dt .meta { font-weight: 400; margin-left: .4rem; }
  .metrics dd { margin: .3rem 0 0; padding-bottom: .9rem; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line); max-width: var(--measure); font-size: var(--fs-md); }
  .metrics .tbl { display: block; margin: .6rem 0; }
  .metrics .bench-runs { font: 400 var(--fs-sm)/1.6 var(--font-mono); color: var(--muted); margin: .4rem 0; overflow-wrap: anywhere; }
</style>
